# Production topology (issue #626).
#
# Committed with placeholders on purpose: hostnames and the preview allowlist
# are deployment facts, not repository facts, and the values that reach a
# cluster must come from the operator's own file. The deploy script copies this
# tree and overwrites this file from NCHAT_PROD_TOPOLOGY_FILE;
# validate_rendered_placeholders then refuses to apply anything still carrying
# a REPLACE_ME_* token, so an unset topology fails before kubectl, not after.
#
# CI renders the committed placeholders as-is to check the manifests' shape.
NCHAT_PROD_HOST=REPLACE_ME_PROD_HOST
NCHAT_PROD_PUBLIC_URL=REPLACE_ME_PROD_PUBLIC_URL
NCHAT_PROD_PREVIEW_ALLOW_CIDR=REPLACE_ME_PROD_PREVIEW_ALLOW_CIDR
# The media plane, which is external. LiveKit runs as one deployment on AWS --
# the same one nchat-dev connects to -- so these two values name a host this
# cluster does not run and cannot derive from NCHAT_PROD_HOST.
#
# The endpoint is confirmed, not open: production uses
#
#   NCHAT_PROD_LIVEKIT_URL=wss://livekit-dev.nic-labs.com
#   NCHAT_PROD_LIVEKIT_CONNECT_SRC="wss://livekit-dev.nic-labs.com https://livekit-dev.nic-labs.com"
#
# The `-dev` in the hostname is historical: that host is the shared media plane,
# serving nchat-dev and both production slots. Caddy terminates TLS on it and
# reverse-proxies signalling to LiveKit on 7880; RTC (7881/TCP, 7882/UDP) is
# handled by the same AWS host. See the runbook's 3b.3.
#
# They stay REPLACE_ME here anyway, for the reason at the top of this file: the
# values that reach a cluster come from the operator's own topology file, and a
# render still carrying a placeholder is refused before kubectl runs. Committing
# the real ones would make this file, rather than that one, the source of truth.
#
# NCHAT_PROD_LIVEKIT_URL is what media-service dials for the API and what it
# returns to the browser as the token response's serverUrl; the LiveKit SDK
# appends its own paths to it, so it is a scheme and host and nothing more.
# Routing it through this deployment's Traefik would put WebRTC signalling
# behind a gateway that has no reason to see it.
#
# NCHAT_PROD_LIVEKIT_CONNECT_SRC is the browser connect-src allowlist for that
# same host, as the complete space-separated value -- both schemes, because the
# SDK dials wss:// and fetches over https://. Kept whole rather than assembled
# from a hostname so the CSP that reaches the browser is the string an operator
# reviewed. Both must name the same host; prod-blue-green-check asserts it.
#
# The credentials for that host are NOT here and are not topology:
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY and LIVEKIT_API_SECRET come from the sealed nchat-secrets.
NCHAT_PROD_LIVEKIT_URL=REPLACE_ME_PROD_LIVEKIT_URL
NCHAT_PROD_LIVEKIT_CONNECT_SRC=REPLACE_ME_PROD_LIVEKIT_CONNECT_SRC
